= Chemical Bonding - Oct 20

== Models of Bonding

*Ionic*
- metal + nonmetal
- electrons transfer, think IE and EA
- each ion forms a noble gas configuration
- melting points = very high

*Covalent*
- nonmetal + nonmetal
- electron sharing
- shared electrons are localized in a bond between the atoms
- melting points = low

*Metallic*
- metal + metal
- electron pool or sea
- electrons are delocalized
- melting points = moderately high
- melting does not break the metallic bond

== Lewis Structures: Representing Valence Electrons in Atoms

*Octet Rule*: Most atoms prefer to have 8 valence electrons

- The group number in the periodic table gives number of valence electrons
- Write the element symbol that represents nucleus and core electrons
- Use dots to represent valence electrons
- Place one dot at a time on each of the 4 sides of the element symbol
- Keep adding dots, pairing them, until all are used up

A tendency to form closed shells is observed during electron transfer. Think of ionization energy, IE, and electron affinity,

== Lattice Energy

Lattice energy is released when a crystalline solid forms.

Why do ionic compounds form?
- Energy is released when bonds are formed in a crystal.

Formation of a crystalline solid releases a large quantity of lattice energy.

*Lattice energy* is required (endothermic) to separate 1 mol of an ionic solid into ions in the gas phase.

Lattice energy is released (exothermic) when 1 mol of an ionic solid is formed from ions in the gas phase.

The magnitude of the lattice energy depends on the charge and size of the ions (Coulomb’s law).

$ "Electrostatic energy" prop ("charge A" times "charge B") / "distance" $

== The Born Haber Cycle

The *Born-Haber cycle* is a series of measurable steps, starting from the elements to ionic compounds (in standard states).

The lattice energy cannot be measured directly but can be estimated using the Born-Haber cycle and Hess’s law.

Example: #ce[LiF]
+ (sublimation) #ce[Li(s) -> Li(g)]
+ (bond energy) $1\/2$ #ce[F2(g) -> F(g)]
+ (Ionization Energy) #ce[Li(g) -> Li+(g) + e-]
+ (Electron Affinity) #ce[F(g) + e- -> F-(g)]
+ #ce[Li+(g) + F-(g) -> LiF(s)]

Together: #ce[Li(s) +] $1\/2$ #ce[F2(g) -> LiF(s)]

== Trends in Lattice Energy

- Ionic size increase, lattice energy decreases
- Ionic charge increase, lattice energy increases
- Increase up right in periodic table

$
  "Electrostatic energy" prop ("cation charge" + "anion charge") / ("cation radius" + "anion radius") prop Delta H^degree_"lattice"
$

== How model Explain Properties

- hard, rigid, brittle solids
  - deformation requires breaking the strong attractive forces that holds ions in specific positions in the crystal
- do not conduct electricity in the solid state, but do conduct when molten or dissolved
  - charged particles are free to move in a liquid or in solution to carry an electric current
- have very high melting points and boiling points
  - requires breaking the strong attractive forces that hold the ions in specific positions in the crystal

